  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Misty with drizzle today ahead of heavier rain \ntonight into Sunday.\n\nLow pressure has continued to bring scattered light showers and \ndrizzle/mist across parts of the Cape and south coast tonight as it \nhas continued trekking northeast, while more interior parts of the \nregion have remained mostly dry. This trend should continue to start \nthe day. Based on latest observations, the low is centered just \nsouth of Nantucket as of the time of writing this discussion. \nGuidance remains in agreement with the general track of this low \nafter it remained further south of Long Island than initially \nforecast; it will curve up and around the Cape and Islands through \nthe day today, moving up into the NH/ME coast into Sunday morning. \nMisty conditions with periods of drizzle remain the main story for \ntoday as a result of prevailing cyclonic flow. Some CAMs hinted at a \nbrief period of showers in the late morning to afternoon hours, with \nsome even showing them passing through the interior. Highs today \nwill likely not get out of the low 70s for southern New England.\n\nHeavier rainfall moves through tonight, generally after midnight, as \nthe low continues on. PWATs remain elevated, approaching 2.0\" \ntowards SE MA and the Cape and Islands. IVT remains favorable for \nheavier showers, though the more anomalous values should remain \nfurther offshore. With regards to rainfall totals, ensembles top out \nat around a 20% chance of totals meeting or exceeding 1\" in 24 hours \nending Sunday over the Cape and Islands. The exception remains the \nGEFS, which continues to be drier than the other ensembles. \nEnsembles have somewhat trended higher totals slightly more offshore \nover the last few runs while deterministic guidance has somewhat \nvaried more from run to run. The HREF PMM has 24 hour totals getting \nto around 0.75\"-1\" over the same area, including into parts of RI \nand SE MA; 48 hour totals could reach 1.5\". High-res guidance \nindicates streaks of higher totals stretching northeast across the \ncoastal plain, but this could change depending on just how close to \nland the low tracks while it cuts around Cape Cod.\n\nOvercast and misty conditions with periods of drizzle remain the \nstory for Sunday as well as the low continues north and a front \nstarts to approach from the west. Ahead of this front, the flow \nturns more southerly to southwesterly. This will advect in more \nmoisture from the south and increase dewpoints into the 70s. PWATs \napproaching 2.0\" and higher will filer in as well. Notably, CAMs \nhave MUCAPE values approaching 1500-1700 J/kg with limited CIN over \nthe interior for Sunday afternoon. 0-6 km shear approaches 50 kt \nwith effective bulk shear around 35 kt. All of these parameters \ncombined with any possible forcing from the front could set the \nstage for some scattered thunderstorms, some of which could turn \nstrong to severe over the interior. With more southerly flow as \nwell, highs Sunday increase back into the upper 70s and low 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier and warmer next week.\n\nSunday's cold front shifts offshore for Monday. Post-frontal \npassage, much drier air and warmer temperatures take hold with more \nwesterly winds, clearing out clouds and dropping dewpoints through \nWednesday. The base of the trough tracks directly overhead into \nTuesday and heights rise behind it as it continues eastward, though \na weak shortwave may track through and bring a low chance of diurnal \nshowers. High pressure continues building into the region into the \nmiddle of next week and flow aloft becomes more zonal. Low pressure \ndeveloping over the Great Lakes towards the second half of the week \nmay play more of a role later in the week, but for the time being, \nno major disturbances are expected through Thursday.",
